WEBVTT

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:04.190
In this place so unreal

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.860
I wait for dawn to come

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.990
Farther and farther, like the sky I saw that day

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.240
What a joke this hope is

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:22.790
I acted as if I had forgotten

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.290
Farther and farther, like the dream I saw that day

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.180
I just want to know the meaning of it

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.300
Within this scattered time

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.140
Along with the breath of ours

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:45.100
And of this pain that never goes away

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:48.610
Even though the roaring of the sea muffles the voices

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.530
Darkness engulfs everything ahead

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.530
The light that passes through our fragile hearts

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.950
Won't go away

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.790
We only relied on our compass

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.920
We believed you

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:14.760
While we stand here

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:19.930
We're searching for the unseen future

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:29.860
Zipang

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.280
Kusaka's words are like narcotics.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.280
Dangerous, yet sweet.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.540
Captain! I will...

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.710
...I will support your decision, Captain!

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.590
Briefing Session next!

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:54.460
We need to come up with the best strategy for this operation!

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.550
Yes!

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:00.890
Interchange

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.200
Lieutenant, where're you going?

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.990
Oh, Lieutenant Satake. What're you doing?

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.330
What about you?

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.580
Are you paying a social visit to the Imperial Soldiers?

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.790
They sent someone over to check us out, right?

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.750
We should return the favor.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.550
I think they're part of some special operations unit.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.090
It's too risky.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:39.050
Don't you want to know what those fellas are thinking?

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.090
Come with me!

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.720
Uh, hold on a second!

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.220
Lieutenant.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.730
You're going over to tell them the truth, aren't you?

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.600
That Japan will lose?!

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:53.730
That this battle is futile?!

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.530
I'm not going to do something like that.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.700
Even if the frontline knew the truth, they still couldn't run.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.620
According to history, this war goes on for another 3 years.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.660
It's not easy to keep fighting knowing you'll lose.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.460
Would you be able to?

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.540
Ignorance is bliss, I say.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.460
I'm the one who wants to know more about them.

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:19.260
What do you want to know?

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.640
About the lifestyle and people from my grandfather's time.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.480
Who's there?

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.230
We're not spies.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.110
Shimizu entering.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.490
What's this?! Why you!!

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.000
Mirai Navigator, and Lieutenant Commander Kohei Oguri.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.840
And I am Mirai Ensign Pilot, Lieutenant Mamoru Satake.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.550
Lieutenant, am I right?

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.720
High ranking officers, right?

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:08.890
My name's Kawamoto.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:10.600
I'm the Petty Chief Officer.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.270
What's your purpose?

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:18.650
I'd like to thank you for helping with the re-supply of our ship.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:22.570
And, I'd like to propose a toast to this ship too!

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:27.370
Though they look the same, they're very different from us.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.370
Yes, they're soldiers from this era who murdered Mori!

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.250
Excuse me! Everyone, please take a seat.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.380
I was born in Hakata, so I'm not used to formality.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:43.340
To meet some fellow Japanese in the Southern Islands...

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:45.640
...this calls for a celebration!

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.100
Bring out the glasses!

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:50.470
Right away!

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.190
This is delicious!

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.570
Bourbon's the American sake. It's made from corn, right?

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.440
I had some at the local "res" with a "geis."

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:05.950
"Res?" "Geis?"

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:09.990
They don't understand what you're saying!

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:11.410
I don't think they have geishas there!

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.370
Yes, there are one or two local geishas.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:15.920
The local geishas.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.290
"Res" refers to restaurants.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:22.300
Could you answer a question, Lieutenant Commander Kohei?

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:25.470
Is there any truth to the rumor that you people come from...

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:27.550
...Japan 60 years in the future?

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.180
It's not so convincing while having a drink but...

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:34.640
...but yes, the rumor's true.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.360
I thought it was some story the Army made up...

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.690
...as a cover for a new warship they built.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.320
It's just like a time machine out of a science-fiction novel!

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:49.620
We have no idea what's going on either.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:52.700
By the time we realized it, we were already here.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.250
We encountered a low pressure front after we reached Midway.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:57.120
Then we were struck by lightning.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.710
So your family's been left behind?

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:01.500
Yes.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.750
I too, have a question.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.760
I'm sure you know the answer, Lieutenant.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.340
What will happen to Japan, in this war?

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.180
Japan's economy will boom after the war.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.230
Mirai itself is the proof of that.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:20.650
A military ship is representative of its country's technology, right?

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.980
High-quality Japanese goods are being sold worldwide...

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:27.110
...and now, Japanese cars are being driven in America.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.360
Japan has become a very prosperous country,

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.580
envied by all the other countries.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:36.450
And all of these, are the fruits of your efforts.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.750
The post-war Japanese people are very grateful to you.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.630
Lieutenant Commander Oguri,

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.590
I understand that Japan will become prosperous.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.220
But Tokyo faced an air raid just this April.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.760
Will mainland Japan really be okay?

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.930
Petty Chief Officer Kawamoto, where's your hometown?

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:57.350
I'm from Hiroshima.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.100
My wife and my daughter live in the city center there.

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:02.230
Hiroshima?!

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:12.280
What is it?

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.530
Nothing. It'll be fine.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:17.410
Is that so?

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:21.710
Alright, let me sing a song to lift our spirits!

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:24.000
That'd be great! It's been a while!

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.460
Yes! Let's do a song!

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.050
A welcome for the two gentlemen!

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:28.710
So, what should we sing?

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.800
Don't sing any military songs this time.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.050
Watanabe, give us the usual!

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:34.430
My pleasure!

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.560
You know, you're a good man.

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:20.890
What?

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:23.730
I can tell that...

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.310
...this war won't be as simple as you make it sound.

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:30.400
Japan is going to experience some very tough times.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.450
Your thoughts are written all over your face.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:36.660
You won't make a good superior but you'd make a good friend.

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:38.450
Even though we're from different eras...

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.830
...I'm happy to have met a soldier like you.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:45.960
Lieutenant Oguri, on behalf of my men, I thank you!

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:49.630
I... er... Hiroshima will...

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.590
Oh, don't you worry.

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:57.090
According to Lieutenant Kusaka, your appearance means...

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.060
...that history's changing.

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.640
Petty Chief Kawamoto, shall we have a round?

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.150
A round?

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.190
Arm wrestling!

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.730
I'll take you on.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:15.360
Sorry to say, but the Imperial Navy won't lose.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.320
Don't you underestimate the SDF either!

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:18.950
By the way, Lieutenant...

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:19.830
Yes?

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:21.910
Who'll give the signal?

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.750
Unbelievable. But that's Oguri for you.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:27.750
That guy can survive in any era.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:34.260
If all goes smoothly, Japan will become a brilliant empire.

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:38.010
If that happened, would you still want to go back?

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:43.310
You can only have one birthplace, right?

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:51.730
Lieutenant Oguri, coming in.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:06.160
You smell! Drinking's forbidden on board.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:08.830
The commander brought it in from Singapore.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:15.090
Lieutenant Kusaka aside, why is Lieutenant Tsuda here too?

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.800
Kusaka would like for him to be present.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:19.840
Oh, I see.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:22.600
Looks like everyone's here.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:25.810
Let's start then. At 0600 tomorrow,

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:28.770
this ship will set sail for Guadalcanal's...

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:32.310
...territorial waters of Solomon.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:35.860
Our primary objective is to prevent...

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:39.450
...the battle between American and Japanese forces.

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:41.780
We also want...

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:46.580
...to reduce the number of Japanese and American deaths.

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.120
Lieutenant Kikuchi, please explain.

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:51.000
Yes.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:55.250
It started off when the Allied Fleet...

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.090
...accompanying the US Marines to Guadalcanal island...

00:10:57.090 --> 00:10:58.840
...was attacked fiercely...

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:02.640
...on the Imperial Navy's Eighth Fleet...

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:05.220
...in the First Battle of Solomon.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.020
The Eighth Fleet destroyed 4 heavy cruisers...

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:12.150
...and dealt heavy blows to one cruiser and two destroyers.

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:15.400
It should have been a victory for Japan...

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:18.690
...but Lieutenant General Gunichi Mikawa...

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:20.360
...feared an allied counterattack...

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:22.450
...and called for a retreat instead.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.740
As a result, the US Marines were able to land...

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:30.830
...and the escort fleet was not neutralized.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:33.040
We need to pay particular attention...

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:34.960
...to the convoy over here.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:36.960
If we can prevent this high-speed convoy...

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:39.340
...from reaching the island...

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:42.380
...we will cut off supplies to the 11 thousand US Marines...

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:45.600
...on the island and leave them totally isolated.

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:50.680
Are you saying that we're going to sink the convoys?

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:53.270
The American troops value the lives of their soldiers...

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:55.770
...and won't leave them stranded for long.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:58.110
They will make a quick retreat.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.610
Accordingly, the war will be avoided.

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:04.870
Sacrifices are inevitable.

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:06.740
Nonetheless, if we can prevent this tragedy...

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.040
...and minimize sacrifice...

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:12.540
...I feel that this will be the best course of action.

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:16.590
So we attack the escort convoys?

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:20.260
Lieutenant, are we gonna use the harpoon on them?!

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:23.680
No. Since they're escorts, they won't be as strong.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:26.430
This ship's main cannon should do the job for us.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:30.100
What're the expected casualties for this attack?

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.520
I'm assuming there are 20 to 30 men on each ship.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:37.440
The lowest possible number would be around 300.

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:40.990
300 lives.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:44.780
We already know what the worst-case scenario is.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:46.620
If we can rescue more than 20 thousand...

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:48.580
...American and Japanese soldiers...

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:50.740
...then this operation's worth a try.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:52.660
Pardon me, but I have something to say.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.620
Launching a rescue operation at the frontline of a battle...

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:57.500
...is just ridiculous.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:00.840
What?!

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.630
Are you saying we should just watch 20 thousand men die?!

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:06.340
It is evident that the Americans intend to occupy Guadalcanal...

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:09.220
...because of its proximity to Australia.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:11.310
They could send supplies by air...

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:13.600
...or do whatever it takes to occupy the island.

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:15.600
So if the battle's inevitable...

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:18.310
...what should we do instead?

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:21.690
You just... You just have to buy us some time.

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:26.410
It's the Japanese who should retreat, not the Americans!

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:28.280
It's not so simple!

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:30.030
This is no time for naive plans!

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:32.200
I will meet with Commander-in-Chief,

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:34.460
Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto, and ask for our troops...

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:37.170
...to be pulled out of Guadalcanal!

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:39.590
What did you say?!

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:41.500
Are you sure that's possible?

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:43.670
The Admiral himself is on board the Battleship Yamato,

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:47.970
which is currently at Truk and not far from Guadalcanal.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:50.300
The Umidori should be able to cover the distance.

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:52.390
If you would kindly prepare the plane for me,

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:53.720
I will set out to convince the Admiral.

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:57.020
Are you saying the Admiral will agree to your proposal?

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:00.520
I cannot make any promises, but it is very possible.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:03.440
I'm sure all of you are just as acquainted...

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.860
...with the Admiral's personality as I am, no?

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:11.620
This is the best suggestion that I have.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:13.950
What do you think, Captain?

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:18.330
I think that Admiral Yamamoto...

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:21.500
...is one of the most intelligent men of his time.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.210
I shall place my hopes on him.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:29.090
Lieutenant Kusaka, I'm counting on you.

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:30.470
Thank you very much.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:33.760
In order to prevent a battle from misunderstanding,

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:36.350
I strongly recommend that an Imperial Navy representative...

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:39.600
...remain on board while I am away.

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:44.360
I'd like to leave Lieutenant Tsuda in your care.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:48.780
A meeting with the Commander-in-Chief...

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:52.240
...you really know how to talk big.

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:03.380
Prepare to depart!

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:04.500
Release tether one!

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:06.170
Releasing!

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:08.720
Advance at low speed!

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:13.800
The engine sounds so different!

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:15.760
Where's all the smoke?!

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:23.770
This is amazing.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:27.400
Petty Chief Kawamoto!

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:50.130
Toshinmaru. Do your best to survive this war.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:53.970
We will now be entering the Allied territorial waters.

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:57.760
All anti-air, anti-sub and anti-ship systems on standby!

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:13.910
Philippines

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:16.280
How is it? Do we have a response?

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:18.330
CIC, Communications.

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:21.460
At present, we do not have any incoming response from Truk.

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:25.040
The Toshinmaru should be there and Officer Kawamoto...

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:27.420
...should be making his report to Headquarters.

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:29.130
After the Ogasawara incident,

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:31.590
I'd prefer not to advance until we receive proper confirmation.

00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:33.630
What do you think? Should we proceed...

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.590
...without verification?

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:39.060
There's no time to spare. We have to move on.

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:49.070
We sincerely hope that you will convince Admiral Yamamoto...

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:53.860
...to withdraw his troops from Guadalcanal.

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:58.160
This ship will proceed alone and buy you as much time...

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:00.910
...as possible to wrap up negotiations.

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:02.870
Good luck to you!

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:08.590
I'll see you again at Guadalcanal.

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:18.350
Take off!

00:17:29.650 --> 00:17:31.690
Do your best, Kusaka.

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:36.530
Masayuki, I thought you didn't want to interfere with history.

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:39.410
I'm surprised you accepted this operation so readily.

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:43.000
I am not as strong as you and Kohei are.

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:45.870
I am afraid of changing history.

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:48.630
If my family and friends were to disappear...

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:50.960
...and we did manage to make it back to our era...

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:53.090
...there wouldn't be a home for me to go back to.

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:56.880
But, that's only a trivial concern.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:02.850
Be it Midway, or Guadalcanal, both are watersheds in history.

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:07.020
This interference won't just change the battle situation.

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:08.400
It isn't Japan's fate alone...

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:10.610
...but the fate of the world that's being decided.

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:16.030
We're creating a world totally different from our 21st Century.

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:21.280
I understand, that it will change the next thousand years.

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:23.540
That's how serious it is.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:29.750
And, by letting Kusaka go, we have set the ball rolling.

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:32.710
Yosuke, can you bear this burden?

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:36.050
The responsibility, for the next one thousand years?

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.840
Or could it be, that like Kohei...

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:44.470
...you think there's a reason we were sent here?

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:45.970
That it's not a mere coincidence...

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:47.770
...but providence instead?

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:56.230
Our objective right now is to end this war quickly...

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:58.530
...and keep casualties to a minimum.

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:00.360
Nothing more, and nothing less.

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:02.780
I'm no politician or philosopher...

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:04.790
...I'm an SDF member.

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:09.460
I do not harbor any doubts when it comes to rescuing a human.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:14.210
Your answer sounds more like one of your beliefs.

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:18.380
You won't have any regrets, regardless of the outcome?

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:23.850
Regular report from the Sea Fowl.

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:27.470
Present altitude: 300 Feet, Speed: 200 knots.

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:30.940
Journey to Truk Islands proceeding smoothly.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:33.190
Time Schedule, All Green.

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:35.610
40 minutes to arrival.

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:39.240
Please don't touch the control panel!

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:42.410
I'm very sorry. I got a little too excited.

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:45.580
I must say though, I'm having a comfortable ride.

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:47.580
Lieutenant Kusaka...

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:50.580
Do you remember that my subordinate died in that seat?

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:54.040
I don't want a second Ogasawara incident.

00:19:56.550 --> 00:19:58.420
Aren't you worried about the confirmation...

00:19:58.420 --> 00:20:00.300
...from the Combined Fleet?

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:03.010
Aside from the flagship Yamato,

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:05.470
many battleships and carriers are parked at Truk.

00:20:05.930 --> 00:20:08.970
Taiwan's Kaoshiong air force should be there too.

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:14.230
Is it too late to worry when we're plunging into a herd of elephants?

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:16.610
Lieutenant Satake.

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:18.980
A light on the panel. It's getting closer.

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:20.820
Looks like we have guests.

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:22.780
2 planes, from 11 o'clock.

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:24.200
Distance 5,500!

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:25.870
We don't have fuel to spare!

00:20:26.200 --> 00:20:27.160
Let's go for it!

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:32.870
I see them! Two fighter planes!

00:20:32.870 --> 00:20:35.880
They're from the Navy. On battle alert.

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:37.750
It's better to be on guard.

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:43.680
It's the banking signal. A symbol of friendship.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:45.800
A welcome from the headquarters.

00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:05.860
He's saying, "Follow me."

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:16.290
There's Truk.

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:23.720
Lieutenant, where are they taking us?

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:25.840
I'm guessing Kaede Island.

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:28.640
It's the airstrip furthest from where the main fleet is parked.

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:31.260
I think they don't want to attract any attention.

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:35.100
Lieutenant Satake, ignore their lead and change course.

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:37.600
We will land over there instead.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:40.650
That's the Yamato!

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:44.280
We might as well make a grand entrance...

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:46.030
...and land right in the middle of fleet headquarters.

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:48.280
Land on Yamato? But which part?

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:52.540
In terms of space and strength, the rear deck should do.

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:57.330
That's interesting. Let's have a go at it then!

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:17.200
Passing through the distant time

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:22.440
Just peacefully

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:34.250
Without losing your way, you walked towards me

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:47.130
How many times have I grasped tightly those small hands of yours?

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:53.730
Not being able to do anything

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:59.810
I kept on praying

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:11.650
Sorrow or agony isn't enough

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:27.340
That's why now as if I wanted to make up... I'm looking at you

00:23:41.390 --> 00:23:44.220
Can a division without land warfare experience...

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:45.930
...carry out its mission successfully?

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:48.940
One Japanese, Takumi Kusaka, has opened his eyes.

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:51.400
We didn't come here for a fire fight.

00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:53.820
They're the children of parents who experienced defeat,

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:55.940
and been brought up in an American influenced society.

00:23:56.320 --> 00:23:57.990
We will become allies in the future.

00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:00.200
Our own lives should come before...

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:04.910
Next Episode: Guadalcanal Island

00:24:00.410 --> 00:24:02.700
20 thousand American and Japanese soldiers.

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:09.960
This story is purely fiction. The names of the
characters and organizations that appear have no
connections with actual people and organizations.
